{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01P1o1jqoYFPy6Lp2Hg33i5h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CfY4xDqziByhycM8c5sJB4","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","input":{"project":"distill_sparse_swiglu","file":"paper_framing.md","max_chars":0},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1779,"cache_read_input_tokens":17117,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1779,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":66,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01AVtF2XfyR1G65a35WeM2LB","session_id":"ea246f80-3204-4c6f-a88a-431a28811afc","uuid":"0145298c-d1e1-4180-a86a-736e6f6ba2ed"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CfY4xDqziByhycM8c5sJB4","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_14/projects/distill_sparse_swiglu/paper_framing.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Paper Framing — distill_sparse_swiglu\\\\n\\\\n## Target Venue\\\\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 2026 cycle)\\\\n- Deadline: 2026-05-25 (ARR submission)\\\\n- Track: Efficient Methods for NLP\\\\n\\\\n## Core Claims\\\\n\\\\n1. **KL-trained sparsity predictors eliminate the need for error compensation networks**: At 50% model-level sparsity on SwiGLU LLMs, a KL-trained contextual predictor (no compensation) matches or exceeds BCE+compensation quality (FastForward-style), eliminating 67.2M compensation parameters (~256MB, 32 layers × bottleneck MLP) and per-layer GEMV overhead at inference. All quality comparisons must be presented alongside inference cost (params, FLOPs, latency) to prevent \\\\\\\"unfair comparison\\\\\\\" attacks. (Headline claim)\\\\n\\\\n2. **Static and contextual corrections compose additively**: SPON's input-independent bias correction (average-case drift) and KL-trained contextual prediction (per-input variation) provide additive quality recovery. Formalized as modular decomposition c = c_s + c_theta(x) where output-space interaction is negligible.\\\\n\\\\n3. **KL advantage grows with sparsity level (testable prediction)**: BCE's per-neuron factorized gradient (mean-field analog) misses joint neuron interactions scaling as O(s^2 D^2). We predict KL-vs-BCE delta follows approximately quadratic curve in sparsity level s — to be validated by 7-level sparsity sweep (plan_008). If empirical scaling deviates from quadratic (linear, log, or phase transition), the actual pattern is itself a finding; abstract/intro will report observed scaling, not assumed quadratic.\\\\n\\\\n## Story Spine\\\\n\\\\n- **Problem**: SwiGLU activation sparsity enables 40-60% neuron pruning at inference, but training-free heuristics (WINA/TEAL) leave a 6+ point quality gap, while learned BCE predictors require auxiliary compensation networks adding ~256MB overhead (FastForward).\\\\n\\\\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: (a) Training-free (WINA, TEAL, R-Sparse): no training cost, but quality gap too large for deployment. (b) Static learned (SPON): zero-overhead bias correction via KL distillation, but input-independent — cannot adapt to per-input neuron importance. (c) Contextual learned (FastForward): BCE predictor + compensation MLP, near-dense quality but inference overhead from compensation network. Gap: no prior work trains the contextual predictor itself with KL, and no work tests SPON + contextual composition.\\\\n\\\\n- **Our framing / headline question**: \\\\\\\"Does output-distribution distillation (KL) make error compensation unnecessary for contextual sparsity prediction?\\\\\\\" The theoretical grounding: BCE gradient dL_BCE/ds_j depends only on neuron j (factorized), while KL gradient dL_KL/ds_j couples all active neurons through d_logits/dh * dh/dm — a mathematical property of the gradient structure, not an analogy. This means KL captures joint interaction effects that BCE misses, especially at high sparsity.\\\\n\\\\n- **Headline result template**: \\\\\\\"On [benchmarks], a KL-trained predictor at 50% model-level sparsity achieves [X] quality (within [Y]% of dense) without compensation, while BCE+compensation achieves [Z] quality but adds 67.2M parameters ([W] ms/token overhead) — demonstrating that output-distribution distillation subsumes error compensation, enabling a simpler, lighter inference pipeline with strictly better efficiency-quality tradeoff.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n## Two-Dimensional Taxonomy (Conceptual Contribution)\\\\n\\\\n|                          | Decomposed (BCE) | Intermediate (MSE) | Coupled (KL) |\\\\n|--------------------------|:-----------------:|:-------------------:|:-------------:|\\\\n| Static                   | —                 | —                   | SPON          |\\\\n| Per-layer                | —                 | ShadowLLM           | —             |\\\\n| Per-token contextual     | FastForward       | —                   | **Ours**      |\\\\n\\\\nPositions prior work and ours on two axes: correction granularity (static -> per-token) x training signal coupling (decomposed/BCE -> coupled/KL). Highlights the empty cell we fill.\\\\n\\\\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\\\\n\\\\n1. **\\\\\\\"Incremental / no new algorithm\\\\\\\"**: Mitigate with formal decomposition framework, quantitative s^2 prediction, and comprehensive four-quadrant outcome analysis showing all results are informative.\\\\n\\\\n2. **\\\\\\\"KL wins only because more compute\\\\\\\"**: Addressed by iso-compute ablation (plan_003) giving BCE equivalent total FLOPs via extended training.\\\\n\\\\n3. **\\\\\\\"Gumbel-sigmoid relaxation, not KL, explains gains\\\\\\\"**: Addressed by STE baseline (plan_002) isolating loss function contribution.\\\\n\\\\n4. **\\\\\\\"Single model / limited generalization\\\\\\\"**: Addressed by Mistral-7B-v0.3 validation (plan_009) with different architecture (GQA + sliding window).\\\\n\\\\n5. **\\\\\\\"Effect size may not hold on downstream tasks\\\\\\\"**: Full benchmark suite (PPL + 5 discriminative + LongBench + MT-Bench) covers all axes.\\\\n\\\\n6. **\\\\\\\"Unfair comparison: BCE+comp close to KL but at higher inference cost\\\\\\\"**: All result tables and figures must co-report quality AND inference cost (compensation params, per-token FLOPs, end-to-end latency). Efficiency-accuracy Pareto frontier (plan_007) is the definitive visualization. Even if BCE+comp matches KL on PPL, KL wins on the Pareto frontier — that IS the claim.\\\\n\\\\n## Venue Fit\\\\n- EMNLP Efficient Methods track: strong fit for activation sparsity + distillation\\\\n- NLP-specific evaluation (LongBench long-context, MT-Bench multi-turn) strengthens EMNLP fit vs pure systems venue\\\\n- Novelty ceiling acknowledged: contribution is systematic composition + rigorous ablation, not new architecture\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 5504, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01AVtF2XfyR1G65a35WeM2LB","session_id":"ea246f80-3204-4c6f-a88a-431a28811afc","uuid":"8ca2523e-e8a8-484a-8e23-52c46ceda896","timestamp":"2026-05-15T17:53:44.864Z"}
