{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qq8PZA9Uwspf57gCTio1Lb","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The R1 review has been submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence 4/5**\n\n**Three critical methodological issues identified:**\n\n1. **BCE baseline fairness (Major).** BCE-only at PPL 28.16 is 2.3x worse than training-free TEAL (12.48), which is anomalous for a trained predictor. The STE ablation only tests KL's relaxation method, never BCE+STE. The project history shows extensive KL debugging (sparsity collapse, normalization bugs, Lagrangian tuning across D003-D006) with no evidence BCE received comparable engineering effort. This confound inflates the reported KL advantage.\n\n2. **Missing MSE-on-logits control (Major).** The gradient coupling property the paper attributes to KL applies to ANY output-level loss. Without an MSE-logits baseline, the paper cannot distinguish \"KL is special\" from \"any coupled loss beats BCE.\" One experiment would resolve this.\n\n3. **Statistical rigor gaps (Major).** Unequal seed counts (5 vs 3), no significance tests across 18 tables, KL seed variance (std 0.87 with seeds ranging 9.14-11.08), and the sparsity sweep using 1 seed per level with R^2=0.66 (linear fit R^2=0.68 is actually higher). The \"compensation is redundant for KL\" claim rests on a 0.45 PPL gap within noise.\n\n**Additional significant issues:** Lagrangian mechanism underspecified for reproduction (W4), SPON+KL composition provides only 0.01 PPL improvement over KL-only undermining Claim 2 (W5), no calibration data sensitivity analysis (W6), and a hyperparameter discrepancy between the paper's stated protocol (batch=8, seq_len=512) and actual code (batch=4, seq_len=2048).\n\n**Path to acceptance:** 2-3 experiments (MSE-logits, BCE+STE, BCE with oracle targets) plus formal significance tests. The core question is well-motivated and the evaluation breadth is above average; the gaps are fixable."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ab6aec36644289b0e (use SendMessage with to: 'ab6aec36644289b0e' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 87840\ntool_uses: 24\nduration_ms: 187480</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ea246f80-3204-4c6f-a88a-431a28811afc","uuid":"f8038857-aec2-41ad-bb30-70cba843d69b","timestamp":"2026-05-15T17:56:11.476Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **methodology soundness and experimental rigor**. Use a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Training Signal Meets Correction Granularity: A Systematic Taxonomy of Learned Activation Sparsity for SwiGLU LLMs\"\n\nThe paper presents a two-dimensional taxonomy for learned activation sparsity: training signal (BCE decomposed vs. KL coupled) × correction granularity (none, static bias, contextual compensation MLP). Evaluated on Llama 3.1 8B and Mistral-7B-v0.3 across 12 benchmarks.\n\n**Key claims**:\n1. BCE+compensation (PPL 9.40±0.19) matches/outperforms KL-only (PPL 9.80±0.87) at 50% sparsity, with 4.6× lower seed variance\n2. SPON+KL corrections are non-additive (additivity ratio = 0.62)\n3. KL–BCE gap does not follow s² scaling (R² = 0.66)\n4. BCE+comp advantage *increases* on Mistral-7B (+1.35 PPL gap vs +0.40 on Llama)\n\n## Experimental Details\n\n**Models**: Llama 3.1 8B (primary), Mistral-7B-v0.3 (generalization)\n**Training**: 1000 steps on WikiText-2 training split, batch size 8, seq len 512, AdamW (lr=3e-4). Compensation phase: 500 additional steps. Target sparsity: 50%.\n**Seeds**: 3-5 per condition (BCE-only 3, BCE+comp 5, KL-only 5, KL+comp 3)\n**Conditions**: BCE-only, BCE+comp, KL-only, KL+comp, SPON, SPON+KL, STE+KL, TEAL, R-Sparse, WINA\n**Benchmarks**: WikiText-2, C4, ARC-c, WinoGrande, MMLU, GSM8K, HumanEval, LongBench (5 task types), MT-Bench (8 categories, GPT-4o judge), RULER (3 task types × 2 context lengths)\n\n## Key Results Tables\n\n**Table 2 (Core ablation)**:\n- Dense: 6.12\n- BCE-only: 28.16±0.22 (+360.1%)\n- BCE+comp: 9.40±0.19 (+53.6%)\n- KL-only: 9.80±0.87 (+60.1%)\n- KL+comp: 8.95±0.13 (+46.2%)\n\n**Table 3 (Per-seed)**:\n- KL-only seeds: s42=9.21, s123=9.25, s456=9.14, s0=10.31, s1=11.08\n- BCE+comp seeds: s42=9.18, s123=9.35, s456=9.52, s0=9.28, s1=9.68\n\n**Table 4 (STE ablation)**: STE+KL 9.89±0.19, Gumbel+KL 9.80±0.87\n**Table 5 (Iso-compute)**: BCE extended (2333 steps, iso-FLOP) = 24.80; BCE+comp (1500 steps) = 9.40\n**Table 7 (SPON+KL composition)**: actual 9.79 vs additive prediction 8.12, additivity ratio 0.62\n**Table 9 (Training-free baselines)**: TEAL 12.48, R-Sparse 14.20, WINA 15.80\n**Table 10 (Full benchmarks)**: BCE+comp avg Δ% = -7.4, KL-only = -8.9, KL+comp = -5.5\n**Table 11 (Inference)**: KL-only 1.35× speedup/48MB, BCE+comp 1.19×/304MB\n**Table 12 (Sparsity sweep)**: R²(Δ~s²)=0.66, R²(Δ~s)=0.68\n**Table 14 (Mistral)**: KL-only 14.15±0.68 (+80.9%), BCE+comp 12.80±0.30 (+63.7%)\n**Table 16 (LongBench)**: KL-only avg drop -18.5%, BCE+comp -14.2%\n**Table 17 (MT-Bench)**: KL-only 5.95, BCE+comp 6.30\n**Table 18 (RULER)**: KL-only 16K avg drop -31.3%, BCE+comp -23.5%\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what SHOULD be covered)\n\nThe full experiment plan includes 12 experiments:\n1. Four-condition ablation @ 50% (plan_001) — COVERED\n2. STE baseline (plan_002) — COVERED\n3. Iso-compute BCE vs KL (plan_003) — COVERED\n4. SPON reimplementation (plan_004) — COVERED\n5. SPON+KL composition (plan_005) — COVERED\n6. Training-free baselines (plan_006) — COVERED\n7. Full benchmark suite + profiling (plan_007) — COVERED\n8. 7-level sparsity sweep (plan_008) — COVERED\n9. Mistral-7B generalization (plan_009) — COVERED\n10. LongBench evaluation (plan_010) — COVERED\n11. MT-Bench evaluation (plan_011) — COVERED\n12. RULER diagnostics (plan_012) — COVERED\n\nAll 12 experiments appear to be covered.\n\n## Your Review Tasks\n\nEvaluate the following aspects in detail:\n\n1. **Methodology soundness**: Is the experimental design appropriate for the claims? Are the comparisons fair? Are there confounding variables?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are the seed counts sufficient? Is variance properly reported? Are comparisons statistically significant?\n3. **Experimental completeness**: Are there missing ablations or controls? Does the paper cover the planned experiments adequately?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce the results?\n5. **Result interpretation**: Are the conclusions well-supported by the data? Are there alternative explanations the authors miss?\n6. **Specific weaknesses in methods/experiments**: Identify concrete issues.\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Detailed comments on methodology\n- Score (1-6) and confidence (1-5)\n- Overall recommendation\n\nBe rigorous and specific. Cite table/section numbers.","agentId":"ab6aec36644289b0e","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The R1 review has been submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence 4/5**\n\n**Three critical methodological issues identified:**\n\n1. **BCE baseline fairness (Major).** BCE-only at PPL 28.16 is 2.3x worse than training-free TEAL (12.48), which is anomalous for a trained predictor. The STE ablation only tests KL's relaxation method, never BCE+STE. The project history shows extensive KL debugging (sparsity collapse, normalization bugs, Lagrangian tuning across D003-D006) with no evidence BCE received comparable engineering effort. This confound inflates the reported KL advantage.\n\n2. **Missing MSE-on-logits control (Major).** The gradient coupling property the paper attributes to KL applies to ANY output-level loss. Without an MSE-logits baseline, the paper cannot distinguish \"KL is special\" from \"any coupled loss beats BCE.\" One experiment would resolve this.\n\n3. **Statistical rigor gaps (Major).** Unequal seed counts (5 vs 3), no significance tests across 18 tables, KL seed variance (std 0.87 with seeds ranging 9.14-11.08), and the sparsity sweep using 1 seed per level with R^2=0.66 (linear fit R^2=0.68 is actually higher). The \"compensation is redundant for KL\" claim rests on a 0.45 PPL gap within noise.\n\n**Additional significant issues:** Lagrangian mechanism underspecified for reproduction (W4), SPON+KL composition provides only 0.01 PPL improvement over KL-only undermining Claim 2 (W5), no calibration data sensitivity analysis (W6), and a hyperparameter discrepancy between the paper's stated protocol (batch=8, seq_len=512) and actual code (batch=4, seq_len=2048).\n\n**Path to acceptance:** 2-3 experiments (MSE-logits, BCE+STE, BCE with oracle targets) plus formal significance tests. The core question is well-motivated and the evaluation breadth is above average; the gaps are fixable."}],"totalDurationMs":187480,"totalTokens":87840,"totalToolUseCount":24,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4774,"cache_read_input_tokens":82542,"output_tokens":523,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4774},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":523,"cache_read_input_tokens":82542,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4774,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4774,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":7,"searchCount":5,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":12}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"abd824c7996585d9b","tool_use_id":"toolu_01AVtF2XfyR1G65a35WeM2LB","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":74178,"tool_uses":21,"duration_ms":229196},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"cb643b98-7add-424e-b5c9-a5a2ef8dbde3","session_id":"ea246f80-3204-4c6f-a88a-431a28811afc"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NiYGtSouG1UFnLv4pYZEpn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TxvYGW3diBRePuGUbgWYxt","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260515T164925\ntier: worst\noverall_score: 3/6 Weak Reject\ntimestamp: 2026-05-15\n---\n\n# R2 Review — Novelty, Significance, Positioning, and Writing Quality\n\n**Paper:** \"Training Signal Meets Correction Granularity: A Systematic Taxonomy of Learned Activation Sparsity for SwiGLU LLMs\"\n**Reviewer:** R2 (Novelty & Related Work focus)\n**Score:** 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n**Confidence:** 4/5\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThe paper presents a two-dimensional taxonomy (training signal × correction granularity) for learned activation sparsity in SwiGLU LLMs and conducts a systematic empirical study filling the previously unoccupied cell (per-token contextual + KL). Contrary to the natural hypothesis motivated by KL's gradient coupling, the paper finds that BCE+compensation (PPL 9.40±0.19) matches or slightly outperforms KL-only (PPL 9.80±0.87) at 50% sparsity on Llama 3.1 8B, while exhibiting 4.6× lower variance. Three negative results are reported: non-additive SPON+KL composition (additivity ratio 0.62), failure of s² scaling for the KL–BCE gap (R²=0.66), and reversal of KL advantage on Mistral-7B.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-structured characterization study with principled ablation design.** The four-condition matrix (loss × compensation), STE isolation (Table 4), iso-compute comparison (Table 5), and SPON composition analysis form a rigorous experimental scaffolding. Each ablation targets a specific confound. This level of systematic control exceeds the norm for the activation sparsity subfield.\n\n- **Honest reporting of negative results.** The paper set out to show KL eliminates compensation and found the opposite. Publishing this with full transparency (additivity ratio 0.62, R²=0.66, direction reversal on Mistral) is scientifically valuable. The reframing from \"KL eliminates compensation\" to \"compensation is the universal mechanism\" is intellectually honest and potentially more useful to practitioners than a positive result would be.\n\n- **Comprehensive evaluation breadth.** 12 benchmarks across perplexity, discriminative tasks, long-context (LongBench, RULER), instruction-following (MT-Bench), inference profiling, and cross-architecture validation. The per-task-type breakdowns (LongBench Table 16, RULER Table 18) are appropriately granular.\n\n- **Variance as a first-class metric.** Reporting that KL-only has 4.6× higher seed variance than BCE+comp (0.87 vs 0.19) is an underappreciated contribution. For deployment, predictability matters as much as mean performance. This finding has immediate practical implications.\n\n- **The taxonomy has genuine organizational value.** Table 1 clearly maps prior work (SPON, ShadowLLM, FastForward) and identifies the empty cell. Even if not a deep theoretical contribution, it provides a useful mental model for the field.\n\n- **Inference cost co-reported with quality.** Table 11 (1.35×/48MB for KL-only vs 1.19×/304MB for BCE+comp) ensures claims are grounded in deployment reality.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Weaknesses\n\n### 3.1 Major\n\n**(W1) The core contribution is a characterization study with no new method, facing a steep novelty bar.**\nThe paper introduces no new architecture, training algorithm, or theoretical framework. The taxonomy is organizational; the experiments combine existing components (Gumbel-sigmoid predictor, KL/BCE losses, SPON bias correction, compensation MLP). For a venue with ~25% acceptance rate, characterization studies must compensate with either (a) deeply surprising findings that overturn community assumptions or (b) analytical depth explaining *why* observed patterns occur. The paper achieves (a) partially (the negative result is mildly surprising, but the prior that \"KL eliminates compensation\" was not widely held — SPON is the only related work, addressing static correction). It does not achieve (b): the discussion of *why* BCE+comp outperforms KL-only (§6) is speculative and does not go beyond restating observations.\n\n**(W2) The gradient coupling argument is presented but then empirically refuted, creating unresolved narrative tension.**\n§3 motivates KL via gradient coupling: KL's gradient couples all neurons through the Jacobian while BCE factorizes. This is mathematically correct but is a property of ANY output-level loss (MSE on logits, JS divergence, reverse KL would all exhibit the same coupling). More importantly, the paper's own results show this coupling does NOT translate to better quality — BCE+comp beats KL-only. The paper attributes this to \"Lagrangian instability\" and \"phase transitions\" (§6) without rigorous evidence. The disconnect between theoretical motivation (§3) and empirical outcome (§5) is the paper's central intellectual weakness. A stronger paper would provide a theoretical explanation for why gradient coupling is necessary but not sufficient, or identify the specific failure mode of KL training that compensation circumvents.\n\n**(W3) Missing MSE-on-logits baseline undermines the gradient coupling narrative.**\nThe gradient coupling property highlighted in §3 applies identically to MSE on output logits. Without this baseline, the paper cannot determine whether KL's specific properties (distributional matching, information-geometric structure) matter, or whether any output-level loss would perform similarly. Given that the paper frames its contribution around the training signal axis of the taxonomy, this omission is critical. The \"Intermediate (MSE)\" column in Table 1 has no per-token contextual entry — this is a gap in the very taxonomy the paper proposes.\n\n**(W4) BCE baseline fairness is questionable.**\nBCE-only achieves 28.16 PPL, dramatically worse than training-free TEAL (~12-13 PPL). A *trained* predictor performing worse than a zero-parameter magnitude heuristic is anomalous. Concerns:\n- Was BCE trained with Gumbel-sigmoid? For a binary target, STE is the natural relaxation. The STE ablation (Table 4) tests STE+KL but never STE+BCE, creating asymmetry.\n- How are BCE oracle targets generated? If by top-k activation magnitude, the targets themselves are suboptimal. KL implicitly discovers better masks.\n- The Lagrangian mechanism gives KL a more sophisticated optimization landscape than vanilla BCE. Did BCE receive comparable engineering attention?\n- In the FastForward paper, BCE+comp reportedly approaches dense quality. The gap here needs explicit comparison with published FastForward numbers.\n\nWithout a BCE+STE condition and transparency about target generation, the BCE baseline's weakness is a confound.\n\n### 3.2 Moderate\n\n**(W5) Related work is severely underdeveloped for a paper claiming a taxonomic contribution.**\nCritical missing citations: Deja Vu (ICML 2023, foundational contextual sparsity predictor), CATS (contextual thresholding for SwiGLU), Minitron (NeurIPS 2024, KL for structured pruning), DIP (MLSys 2025, predictor-free dynamic pruning for SwiGLU), Gumbel-Softmax foundations (Jang et al. 2017, Maddison et al. 2017), L0 regularization (Louizos et al. 2018), Sparsing Law (Luo et al. 2024), ReLU Strikes Back/TurboSparse/ProSparse (activation function replacement), MoE routing literature, and knowledge distillation beyond Hinton 2015.\n\n**(W6) Lagrangian sparsity enforcement mechanism is underspecified.**\nKL training collapsed to 0% sparsity, required normalization fixes, had a bug producing false positives, and took multiple iterations to stabilize. Yet the paper does not fully specify lambda initialization, update schedule, clamping bounds, or sensitivity analysis. This is critical for reproducibility and creates an asymmetry in training infrastructure between KL and BCE.\n\n**(W7) Statistical rigor is insufficient for the claims made.**\n- Unequal seed counts (3 vs 5) across conditions with no justification.\n- The headline comparison (9.40±0.19 vs 9.80±0.87) has overlapping confidence intervals. No formal significance test is reported.\n- Sparsity sweep uses 1 seed per level. R²=0.66 with 7 single-seed points cannot distinguish between functional forms. No AIC/BIC comparison provided.\n- Additivity ratio 0.62 reported without confidence intervals or statistical test for deviation from 1.0.\n\n**(W8) The \"phase transitions vs scaling laws\" framing (§6) is speculative.**\nNo phase transition is demonstrated (e.g., sharp discontinuity), no theoretical mechanism is proposed, and \"phase transition\" is used loosely. The discussion reads as post-hoc rationalization of R²=0.66.\n\n### 3.3 Minor\n\n**(W9)** Only 7-8B scale tested. The \"compensation is universal\" claim is validated at a single model scale.\n\n**(W10)** Calibration data sensitivity unexamined. Training uses WikiText-103, evaluation on WikiText-2. No domain sensitivity analysis.\n\n**(W11)** 512-token training sequences acknowledged but not analyzed. LongBench/RULER degradation could be a training artifact.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **What happens with MSE on output logits as the training loss?** This is the single most informative missing experiment.\n\n2. **Was BCE trained with Gumbel-sigmoid relaxation?** If so, what happens with STE+BCE?\n\n3. **How are BCE oracle targets generated?** Top-k by activation magnitude? How do KL's implicitly learned masks differ?\n\n4. **The headline PPL gap (9.40 vs 9.80) falls within overlapping standard deviations. Can you provide a formal significance test?**\n\n5. **Why is BCE-only (28.16 PPL) worse than training-free TEAL?** If this reflects a genuine phenomenon, it deserves analysis. If inadequate tuning, it undermines the comparison.\n\n6. **What are the Lagrangian hyperparameters?** How sensitive is KL training to these settings?\n\n7. **At what sparsity level does KL-only start to need compensation?**\n\n8. **Can you explain the Mistral direction reversal mechanistically?** What specific property of GQA + sliding window drives this?\n\n---\n\n## 5. Detailed Comments on Novelty and Positioning\n\nThe paper sits at the intersection of two established techniques: KL distillation (standard since Hinton 2015) and learned sparsity prediction (standard since Deja Vu 2023). SPON already demonstrated KL works for sparsity in SwiGLU LLMs. Testing KL for contextual prediction is the natural next step the taxonomy itself identifies. The gradient coupling analysis follows trivially from the chain rule and applies to any output-level loss.\n\nThe most genuinely novel contribution is the negative result: compensation is more robust than gradient coupling. However, impact is weakened by: (a) narrow effect size (9.40 vs 9.80, potentially non-significant), (b) unresolved BCE fairness concerns, and (c) lack of mechanistic explanation.\n\nFraming as \"characterization study\" is the correct strategic choice, but characterization is incomplete: MSE-logits cell is empty, related work does not comprehensively map the field, and discussion lacks analytical depth.\n\nSuccessful characterization studies at top venues typically have much larger scope, deeply surprising findings, or actionable causal guidelines. This paper has moderate scope, a mildly surprising finding with caveats, and limited causal analysis.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Writing Quality\n\n- Organization is clear. Taxonomy-first structure followed by systematic experiments is logical.\n- Claims are mostly well-calibrated, except the \"phase transition\" speculation in §6.\n- Narrative arc has a bait-and-switch quality: §1/§3 build up the KL hypothesis, then §5 knocks it down. Cleaner to frame the question as genuinely open from the start.\n- Table density is high (18 tables in 11 pages). Several tables could be merged.\n- Discussion section (§6) is the weakest: \"phase transitions\" and \"Lagrangian instability\" invoked without evidence.\n- Bibliography is too thin (~8 references for a taxonomy paper).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Score and Recommendation\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n**Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Justification:** The paper asks a good question (does KL training eliminate compensation?) and provides an honest, well-structured empirical answer (no). Evaluation breadth is commendable and reporting negative results has scientific value. However, three converging problems prevent acceptance: (1) thin novelty (loss function swap in existing pipeline, organizational taxonomy), (2) unresolved experimental concerns (missing MSE-logits baseline, BCE fairness, insufficient statistical rigor for narrow effect size), and (3) inadequate analytical depth (no mechanistic explanation for compensation's advantage, speculative discussion, severely underdeveloped related work).\n\n**What would change the score:**\n- MSE-logits baseline + BCE+STE control resolving fairness: +0.5 to +1.0\n- Mechanistic explanation for compensation's advantage (not speculation): +0.5\n- Comprehensive related work (20+ references): +0.5\n- Formal significance tests confirming 9.40 vs 9.80 gap: +0.5\n- If BCE baseline found unfairly weak: −1.0 (to 2/6 Reject)\n\n---\n\n## 8. Missing Experiments (Priority-Ordered)\n\n1. **MSE-on-logits baseline** — Highest priority. Distinguishes \"KL is special\" from \"any output-level loss beats BCE.\"\n2. **BCE+STE baseline** — Ensures BCE uses its natural relaxation.\n3. **BCE with KL-optimal targets** — Disentangles loss from target quality.\n4. **Per-layer PPL decomposition** — Which layers drive the KL-BCE gap?\n5. **Formal significance tests** — Paired bootstrap on headline; AIC/BIC for scaling fit.\n6. **Lagrangian sensitivity analysis** — Required for reproducibility.\n7. **Predictor rank sensitivity** — At least one alternative (r=64 or r=256).","project":"distill_sparse_swiglu","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260515T164925","overall_score":"3/6 Weak Reject"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":706,"cache_read_input_tokens":72870,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":706,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01AVtF2XfyR1G65a35WeM2LB","session_id":"ea246f80-3204-4c6f-a88a-431a28811afc","uuid":"eee940ae-5612-446f-8163-3d198e437a92"}
